  "problem": "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1$ for $x>0?$",
  "worked_solution": "Rearranging and Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+C,\\,x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-1,0,2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011667/assets/8e047c3c27a000af943f50499f8c7559acceeb78cc006e796c7d353729991517.png)",
